---

## 0. Purpose

Phase 06E defines the canonical application shell for GrowFlow.

The shell is the persistent structural frame surrounding the product's pages and workflows.

It establishes:

- global top navigation
- GrowFlow identity placement
- public navigation
- Workplace selector
- Login / Sign Up behavior
- profile access
- notifications
- global search
- authenticated sidebar
- sidebar collapse behavior
- mobile navigation drawer
- breadcrumbs
- content viewport
- independent scrolling
- responsive shell behavior
- route integration
- role-aware navigation
- permission-aware navigation
- loading behavior
- session behavior
- notification behavior
- search behavior
- shell accessibility
- shell state management
- shell observability
- shell testing
- shell implementation boundaries

The shell is shared across the entire GrowFlow website.

---

# 1. Shell Philosophy

GrowFlow uses:

> **One application shell + one design language + role-specific information architecture.**

The shell must feel like one product regardless of whether the user is:

```text
PUBLIC
BUILD
SUPERVISE
GOVERN
```

Role identity is communicated through:

- navigation destinations
- workspace context
- content
- permissions
- data

It is not communicated through separate visual themes.

---

# 2. Shell Architecture

Canonical structure:

```text
Browser
  ↓
GrowFlow Global Shell
  ├── Top Navigation
  │    ├── Brand
  │    ├── Public Links
  │    ├── Workplace
  │    ├── Search
  │    ├── Notifications
  │    ├── Profile
  │    ├── Login
  │    └── Sign Up
  │
  └── Authenticated Workspace
       ├── Sidebar
       └── Main Content
            ├── Breadcrumbs
            ├── Page Header
            └── Page Content
```

---

# 3. Shell Layers

The shell consists of five conceptual layers:

```text
Layer 1 — Global Browser/Application Frame
Layer 2 — Top Navigation
Layer 3 — Workplace Navigation
Layer 4 — Role Sidebar
Layer 5 — Page Content
```

Each layer has a distinct responsibility.

---

# 4. Global Shell Rules

The shell must:

1. remain visually consistent across routes
2. preserve user orientation
3. expose appropriate navigation
4. respect authorization
5. support deep links
6. preserve browser navigation
7. support responsive layouts
8. support keyboard navigation
9. support dark mode
10. support reduced motion
11. tolerate loading states
12. tolerate session expiration
13. tolerate API failures
14. never become a security boundary
15. never own canonical business state

---

# 5. Public Shell

Public pages use the global top navigation but do not use the authenticated application sidebar.

Public structure:

```text
Top Navigation
      ↓
Public Page
      ↓
Footer
```

Applicable pages:

```text
/
 /features
 /documentation
 /documentation/:slug
 /contact
```

