# 10. Brand Placement

GrowFlow brand mark should be positioned at the left edge of the top navigation.

Desktop:

```text
[GrowFlow mark/wordmark]
```

Collapsed/sidebar states may use the standalone brand mark where space requires.

The accepted brand direction is:

```text
Connected Flow + Abstract G
```

---

# 11. Brand Interaction

Clicking the GrowFlow brand should have a predictable destination.

For authenticated users:

```text
brand → current workplace overview/dashboard
```

For public users:

```text
brand → /
```

The exact role destination is:

```text
BUILD      → /student/dashboard
SUPERVISE  → /mentor/overview
GOVERN     → /admin/overview
```

---

# 12. Workplace Selector

The Workplace selector is a canonical global shell control.

It exposes:

```text
BUILD
SUPERVISE
GOVERN
```

The selector must communicate the user's available workspace contexts.

---

# 13. Workplace Selector — Logged Out

For logged-out users, Workplace selection acts as an entry-point selector.

```text
BUILD
  → Student Sign In

SUPERVISE
  → Mentor Sign In

GOVERN
  → Protected Admin Entry
```

Admin must not have a public registration path.

---

# 14. Workplace Selector — Logged In

For authenticated users:

- only authorized workplaces are actionable
- unavailable workplaces should not appear as usable options
- role switching must trigger authorization resolution
- the selector must not grant privileges

Possible behavior:

```text
Click Workplace
    ↓
Resolve available workspaces
    ↓
User selects workspace
    ↓
Authorization check
    ↓
Navigate to workspace overview
```

---

# 15. Workplace Destination Mapping

Canonical destinations:

```text
BUILD
→ /student/dashboard

SUPERVISE
→ /mentor/overview

GOVERN
→ /admin/overview
```

If the user is already inside a compatible workspace and selects the same workplace, do not perform unnecessary navigation.

---

# 16. Role Resolution

Role/workplace resolution follows:

```text
Session
 ↓
Identity
 ↓
Application Role Resolution
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
Workspace Availability
 ↓
Navigation
```

The frontend must not infer authority solely from client-side role state.

---

# 17. Multiple Authorized Workplaces

If a user legitimately has multiple authorized roles:

- Workplace selector exposes authorized roles
- current role is visually indicated
- switching preserves no invalid resource context
- role-incompatible deep links are rejected safely
- switching should normally land on the selected workplace overview

Do not assume all accounts have multiple roles.

---

# 18. Login

Logged-out top navigation exposes:

```text
Login
```

Login is a distinct action from Sign Up.

Selecting Login must lead to role-specific authentication selection or the appropriate authentication destination.

Canonical student login:

```text
/auth/student/sign-in
```

Canonical mentor login:

```text
/auth/mentor/sign-in
```

Admin:

```text
protected admin entry
```

---

# 19. Sign Up

Logged-out top navigation exposes:

```text
Sign Up
```

Student:

```text
/auth/student/register
```

Mentor:

```text
/auth/mentor/register
```

Admin has no public signup.

---

# 20. Deep-Link Preservation

When authentication is required for a protected route:

```text
Protected Route
    ↓
Authentication
    ↓
Role Resolution
    ↓
Authorization
    ↓
Restore Intended Route
```

A `returnTo`-style mechanism may preserve the intended route.

The restored destination must still pass authorization.

---

# 21. Profile

Authenticated top navigation includes profile access.

Profile destination:

```text
/profile
```

Profile menu may provide:

```text
Profile
Settings
Current workplace
Sign out
```

Additional actions may be introduced later if required.

---

# 22. Profile Menu Rules

Profile menu should:

- be compact
- clearly identify the signed-in user
- expose account-level actions
- avoid excessive navigation
- remain keyboard accessible

Sign-out must be explicit and reliable.

---

# 23. Settings

Canonical settings:

```text
/settings
/settings/profile
/settings/preferences
```

Settings belong to the account/user experience rather than project state.

---

# 24. Notifications

Authenticated top navigation includes notifications.

Primary destination:

```text
/notifications
```

Notifications may originate from:

- project events
- mentor actions
- help requests
- AI executions
- document processing
- project changes
- system events

---

# 25. Notification Indicator

Unread notifications may be indicated by:

- count
- dot
- visual weight
- accessible label

Do not rely on color alone.

Avoid unnecessarily large notification counters.

---

# 26. Notification Preview

A compact preview may be presented from the top navigation.

Preview should support:

- recent notifications
- unread state
- timestamp
- concise context
- deep link
- view all

The preview is a contextual surface; the notification center is the canonical collection view.

---

# 27. Notification Deep Links

Notifications should navigate to canonical resources.

Examples:

```text
Task notification
→ /student/projects/:projectId/tasks/:taskId

Blueprint notification
→ /student/projects/:projectId/blueprint

Project change
→ /student/projects/:projectId/changes/:changeId
```

Notification routes must remain authorization-aware.

---

# 28. Global Search

Global search route:

```text
/search
```

Search is role-aware and authorization-aware.

Search may cover:

- projects
- tasks
- milestones
- risks
- documents
- people
- groups
- activity
- other authorized resources

---

# 29. Search Trigger

Top navigation should expose a recognizable search affordance.

Desktop may support:

```text
Search
⌘K / Ctrl+K
```

where keyboard shortcut behavior is implemented.

Mobile may use an icon/button that opens the search interface.

---

# 30. Search Security

Search results must be filtered by backend authorization.

The frontend must never assume:

```text
hidden result = unauthorized
```

The backend remains authoritative.

---

# 31. Search States

Search supports:

```text
idle
focused
typing
loading
results
noResults
error
```

Long-running or remote search must communicate loading.

---

# 32. Sidebar Architecture

The authenticated sidebar is role-specific navigation inside the unified visual system.

Structure:

```text
Sidebar
├── Workspace identity/context
├── Primary navigation
├── Secondary navigation where required
└── Bottom utility area where appropriate
```

---

