---

# 36. Sidebar Dimensions

Default:

```text
248px
```

Collapsed:

```text
72px
```

Desktop implementation should stay within the Phase 06C guidance.

---

# 37. Sidebar Collapse

Desktop users may collapse the sidebar.

Collapsed mode:

- preserves navigation
- shows icons
- uses tooltips where needed
- maintains selected state
- does not disappear entirely

The user should retain orientation.

---

# 38. Sidebar Collapse Persistence

Sidebar preference may be persisted as UI preference.

It must not affect:

- permissions
- backend state
- project state
- canonical routing

---

# 39. Sidebar Selected State

The active navigation item must be obvious.

Use:

- subtle accent treatment
- typography
- background
- icon treatment

Do not use a loud full-color block.

---

# 40. Sidebar Sections

Where navigation becomes large, use semantic section grouping.

Section labels should be:

- concise
- readable
- visually subordinate

Avoid too many sections.

---

# 41. Sidebar Mobile Behavior

On mobile:

```text
Top Navigation
      ↓
Menu Trigger
      ↓
Navigation Drawer
```

The persistent desktop sidebar becomes a drawer.

---

# 42. Mobile Navigation Drawer

Drawer must support:

- open
- close
- overlay
- focus management
- escape
- current route
- workspace context
- navigation

Closing the drawer should return focus to the triggering control.

---

# 43. Content Area

Authenticated shell content must scroll independently from the global top navigation.

Recommended structure:

```text
Viewport
├── Fixed/Sticky TopNav
└── Workspace Region
     ├── Sidebar
     └── Scrollable Content
```

---

# 44. Independent Scrolling

Primary content should own vertical scrolling where appropriate.

The shell must avoid:

- nested unnecessary scroll containers
- page-wide horizontal overflow
- scroll traps
- sidebar/content scroll conflicts

---

# 45. Horizontal Overflow

The shell must prevent accidental horizontal overflow.

Exceptions are intentional component-level cases such as:

- wide tables
- code blocks
- timeline views

Those components should own their horizontal scrolling.

---

# 46. Main Content Container

Main content consumes Phase 06C layout tokens.

Typical page gutters:

```text
Desktop: 32–48px
Tablet: 24–32px
Mobile: 16–20px
```

Page-specific layouts may use approved variations.

---

# 47. Breadcrumbs

Breadcrumbs appear where resource depth benefits orientation.

They should:

- reflect canonical route hierarchy
- be keyboard accessible
- support back navigation
- remain concise

They are not required on every page.

---

# 48. Page Header

The shell provides the structural region for a page header.

A page header may contain:

```text
Breadcrumbs
Title
Description
Status
Actions
```

The page itself owns the content.

The shell should not hardcode page-specific business information.

---

# 49. Shell / Page Boundary

The shell owns:

- navigation
- global actions
- viewport structure
- user context
- role context

The page owns:

- page data
- domain workflows
- page-specific actions
- page-specific states

---

# 50. Shell / Feature Boundary

Feature components may consume shell-provided context:

```text
current user
current role
navigation state
theme
viewport state
notification state
search state
```

They should not mutate shell internals without an explicit contract.

---

# 51. Shell State Model

The shell uses three major state categories from the frontend architecture.

## Server State

Examples:

- current user
- authorized workplaces
- unread notification count
- navigation permissions where exposed by backend

## UI State

Examples:

- sidebar collapsed
- search open
- profile menu open
- workplace menu open
- notification preview open
- mobile drawer open
- theme preference

## Streaming State

Examples:

- notification delivery
- live execution updates
- processing state indicators

---

# 52. Shell State Ownership

Recommended:

```text
Application Shell State
├── Navigation UI State
├── User/session context
├── Notification state
├── Search state
└── Workspace context
```

Avoid storing project domain data in the shell.

---

# 53. Shell State Persistence

Appropriate persisted UI preferences:

- sidebar collapse
- appearance preference
- selected interface preference where applicable

Do not persist:

- authorization decisions
- project lifecycle
- task status
- AI canonical execution state

---

# 54. Session State

Shell must respond to:

```text
authenticated
sessionRefreshing
sessionExpired
signedOut
```

Session state comes from the authentication/application integration.

---

# 55. Session Expiration

If session expires:

```text
Current page
    ↓
Session expiration detected
    ↓
Preserve safe return intent
    ↓
Authentication
    ↓
Re-authorize
    ↓
Return if allowed
```

Sensitive information must not leak into unsafe return parameters.

---

# 56. Unauthorized Shell Access

A user may reach a route they cannot access.

Expected behavior:

```text
Route requested
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
403 / appropriate access state
```

Do not simply hide the entire application because one resource is forbidden.

---

# 57. Shell Loading

Initial shell loading may need:

- top navigation skeleton
- sidebar skeleton
- user context placeholder

Avoid flashing an incorrect role navigation before role resolution completes.

---

# 58. Role Resolution Loading

During role resolution:

```text
Authenticated
    ↓
Resolving workspace
    ↓
Loading correct shell
```

The frontend should avoid:

```text
Student sidebar
    ↓
flash
    ↓
Mentor sidebar
```

unless unavoidable.

---

# 59. Navigation Transition

Navigation should feel immediate.

For normal route transitions:

- update route promptly
- show local page loading
- preserve shell
- avoid full application reloads

---

# 60. Long-Running Workflow Navigation

Leaving a page must not terminate long-running backend work.

Example:

```text
Blueprint Generation
    ↓
User navigates elsewhere
    ↓
Backend job continues
    ↓
Event persists
    ↓
Notification delivered
    ↓
User returns
```

The shell is not responsible for running the job.

---

# 61. SSE Integration

The shell may subscribe to global event streams for:

- notifications
- relevant processing events
- system messages

SSE is a delivery mechanism.

It is not canonical state.

---

# 62. SSE Reconnection

On disconnect:

```text
Disconnect
 ↓
Reconnect
 ↓
Resume delivery where supported
 ↓
Reconcile canonical state
```

The shell should not assume every missed event was received.

---

# 63. Notification Reconciliation

After reconnection:

```text
SSE
 ↓
event
 ↓
update notification state
 ↓
reconcile unread count
```

If event history cannot guarantee correctness, fetch canonical notification state.

---

# 64. Theme Integration

Shell supports:

```text
Light
Dark
```

The shell must consume Phase 06C theme tokens.

Theme does not alter:

- role identity
- route structure
- permissions
- navigation hierarchy

---

# 65. Reduced Motion

Shell motion should be subtle.

Animated areas:

- sidebar collapse
- mobile drawer
- menus
- notifications
- search palette
- theme transitions where appropriate

Reduced-motion preference overrides decorative transitions.

---

# 66. Top Navigation Responsive Behavior

## Desktop

Show full navigation where space allows.

## Tablet

Prioritize:

```text
Brand
Workplace
Search
Notifications
Profile
```

Secondary public links may collapse.

## Mobile

Prioritize:

```text
Brand
Search/menu
Notifications
Profile
```

Secondary navigation moves into appropriate menus/drawers.

---

# 67. Public Navigation Responsive Behavior

Desktop:

```text
Brand | Features | Documentation | Contact | Workplace | Login | Sign Up
```

Mobile may consolidate public links into a menu.

Login and Sign Up remain discoverable.

---

# 68. Authenticated Mobile Shell

Recommended:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Brand   Search  Bell  Menu   │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│                              │
│          Content             │
│                              │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

Menu opens role-specific navigation.

---

# 69. Shell Z-Index Strategy

Use a small semantic layering system.

Conceptual order:

```text
base content
sticky shell
dropdown/popover
drawer
modal
critical overlay
```

Do not assign arbitrary huge z-index values to every component.

---

# 70. Shell Surface Treatment

Shell should remain visually quiet.

Use:

- subtle background difference
- subtle border
- minimal elevation
- restrained active states

Avoid:

- glassmorphism everywhere
- heavy shadows
- neon outlines
- decorative gradients

---

# 71. Sticky Behavior

Top navigation may remain sticky.

If sticky:

- content must account for its height
- focus navigation must not hide content beneath it
- anchor scrolling must account for header offset

---

# 72. Sidebar Sticky Behavior

Desktop sidebar may remain fixed/sticky while content scrolls.

It should not create an independent vertical scroll unless the navigation itself exceeds viewport height.

---

# 73. Accessibility — Shell

Shell must provide:

- skip-to-content
- semantic landmarks
- accessible navigation labels
- keyboard navigation
- visible focus
- correct current-page indication
- accessible menu behavior
- drawer focus management
- screen-reader state announcements where needed

---

# 74. Landmark Structure

Recommended:

```html
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<aside>
```

Use landmarks semantically.

Do not create multiple ambiguous navigation landmarks without labels.

---

# 75. Skip Navigation

Authenticated and public layouts should support a skip-to-content mechanism.

Example:

```text
Skip to main content
```

It should become visible on keyboard focus.

---

# 76. Current Navigation

Active route should use appropriate semantic state.

Where applicable:

```text
aria-current="page"
```

must represent the current destination.

---

# 77. Workplace Selector Accessibility

Must communicate:

- current workspace
- available workspaces
- expanded/collapsed state
- selected workspace
- disabled/unavailable state where relevant

Keyboard:

```text
Enter / Space
Arrow keys
Escape
```

as appropriate for the implemented menu pattern.

---

# 78. Search Accessibility

Search should provide:

- accessible label
- focus
- keyboard shortcuts where supported
- result count/status where useful
- selected result indication
- no-results message

---

# 79. Notification Accessibility

Unread count should be announced meaningfully.

Do not make screen readers interpret only a decorative dot.

Notification previews must support keyboard navigation.

---

# 80. Sidebar Accessibility

Collapsed sidebar must still expose accessible names for icon-only navigation.

Tooltips are supplementary, not the sole accessible label.

---

# 81. Mobile Drawer Accessibility

When opened:

```text
focus enters drawer
```

When closed:

```text
focus returns to trigger
```

Escape should close the drawer.

Background interaction should be appropriately controlled.

---

# 82. Shell Error Handling

Shell-level errors include:

- session error
- navigation data error
- notification connection failure
- search service failure
- workspace resolution failure

These should not unnecessarily destroy page content.

---

# 83. Search Failure

Search failure should show:

```text
Search unavailable
Try again
```

without taking down the application.

---

# 84. Notification Failure

If live notifications fail:

- preserve existing notification state
- show non-disruptive connection state where useful
- retry/reconnect
- do not block core navigation

---

# 85. Workspace Resolution Failure

If authorized workspace resolution fails:

- avoid showing incorrect navigation
- preserve authenticated identity state
- show recovery state
- retry
- fail safely if necessary

---

# 86. Shell Offline / Network State

If network connectivity changes:

- preserve current UI where possible
- indicate operations that cannot complete
- avoid falsely confirming mutations
- allow retry
- reconcile when connection returns

---

# 87. Navigation Security

Navigation is not authorization.

A hidden item does not mean:

```text
user is forbidden because UI hid it
```

Authorization is enforced by backend/application security.

---

# 88. Route Guarding

Frontend route guards may:

- improve UX
- prevent unnecessary rendering
- redirect unauthenticated users

They must not replace backend authorization.

---

# 89. Deep-Link Security

Every deep link must be revalidated.

Example:

```text
/student/projects/project-123
```

must not become accessible merely because the user knows the URL.

---

# 90. Share Integration

Share interactions use canonical URLs.

The shell may provide share actions through profile/resource actions where appropriate.

Shared links must remain authorization-aware.

---

# 91. Browser Navigation

The shell must preserve:

- back
- forward
- refresh
- deep links
- direct URL entry

Route state should remain meaningful after refresh.

---

# 92. Query Parameters

Use query parameters for:

- search query
- filters
- sorting
- pagination
- display mode

Do not encode resource identity solely in query parameters when a canonical route is appropriate.

---

# 93. Sidebar and URL State

Sidebar selection should derive from route state where possible rather than maintaining an independent conflicting navigation state.

---

# 94. Search and URL State

Global search route may preserve:

```text
/search?q=...
```

Filters or result modes should remain shareable where useful.

---

# 95. Notification and URL State

Opening a notification preview is UI state.

Navigating to the target resource is route state.

Do not make notification preview itself a canonical business resource route unless required.

---

